    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>If you are a first time home buyer in Texas, you have more help available than most people realize: two state agencies offer down payment assistance of up to 5% of your loan, and you can pair it with an FHA, VA, USDA or conventional loan. The rest comes down to knowing the Texas-specific steps, especially the option period, and budgeting for the costs that show up after the down payment.</p>
<p>This guide walks through the programs, the loans, the buying process from pre-approval to keys, and the money you need at each stage.</p>

<h2>Who counts as a first-time home buyer in Texas</h2>
<p>You do not have to be buying your first home ever. For the state programs, a first-time buyer is someone who has not owned or had an ownership interest in a principal residence during the last three years. If you sold a house in 2021 and have rented since, you likely qualify again.</p>
<p>There are also exceptions. Qualified veterans and buyers purchasing in federally designated targeted areas can skip the first-time requirement on some programs. And several programs have no first-time requirement at all, which we cover below.</p>

<h2>Texas first-time home buyer programs: TSAHC and TDHCA</h2>
<p>Texas runs its homebuyer assistance through two organizations. Both work through participating lenders, so you apply when you apply for your mortgage, not with the state directly. Both cap help at 5% of the loan amount and both run on limited funds.</p>

<h3>TSAHC: Homes for Texas Heroes and Home Sweet Texas</h3>
<p>The Texas State Affordable Housing Corporation (TSAHC) offers two home loan programs with down payment and closing cost help of up to 5% of the loan amount:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Homes for Texas Heroes</strong> is for full-time public school educators, police and public security officers, firefighters and EMS personnel, veterans and active military, corrections officers, and nursing or allied health faculty.</li>
<li><strong>Home Sweet Texas</strong> is for buyers who do not work in a hero profession but meet income limits.</li>
</ul>
<p>TSAHC assistance comes in three forms: a grant that never has to be repaid, a deferred forgivable second lien that is forgiven after three years if you do not sell, refinance or move out, and a deferred repayable second lien that you pay back when you sell, refinance or pay off the first mortgage. Both programs serve first-time and repeat buyers.</p>
<p>TSAHC also offers a Mortgage Credit Certificate (MCC), a federal tax credit equal to a percentage of the mortgage interest you pay each year. As of 2026, TSAHC has discontinued its stand-alone MCC indefinitely; you can still get one alongside a TSAHC loan while funds last.</p>

<h3>TDHCA: My First Texas Home and My Choice Texas Home</h3>
<p>The Texas Department of Housing and Community Affairs (TDHCA) runs the Texas Homebuyer Program:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>My First Texas Home</strong> is for first-time buyers and qualified veterans. It pairs a 30-year fixed-rate mortgage with optional assistance of 2% to 5% of the loan, structured as a 0% interest, non-amortizing second lien that is deferred for 30 years and repaid when you sell, refinance or pay off the loan.</li>
<li><strong>My Choice Texas Home</strong> has no first-time buyer requirement. It offers the same repayable second lien or, on some loan types, a 3-year forgivable second lien.</li>
<li><strong>Texas Mortgage Credit Certificate</strong> is available to first-time buyers and qualified veterans, either combined with a TDHCA loan or as a stand-alone certificate with an outside mortgage.</li>
</ul>
<p>TDHCA requires an approved homebuyer education course, and its program materials list a 620 minimum credit score. Income and purchase price limits vary by county, so check the current Harris, Fort Bend or Montgomery County limits with a participating lender.</p>

<h3>TSAHC vs TDHCA at a glance</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>TSAHC</th><th>TDHCA</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Max assistance</td><td>Up to 5% of loan</td><td>Up to 5% of loan</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assistance type</td><td>Grant, 3-year forgivable lien or repayable lien</td><td>30-year deferred repayable lien; 3-year forgivable on some My Choice loans</td></tr>
<tr><td>First-time required?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes for My First Texas Home; no for My Choice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Special track</td><td>Homes for Texas Heroes</td><td>Veteran and targeted-area exceptions</td></tr>
<tr><td>MCC</td><td>Only with a TSAHC loan (stand-alone discontinued)</td><td>Combined or stand-alone</td></tr>
<tr><td>How to apply</td><td>Participating lender</td><td>Participating lender</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>One trade-off to know: assisted loans often carry a slightly higher interest rate than an unassisted loan. Ask your lender to quote both so you can compare the monthly payment against the cash you save upfront.</p>

<h2>Choosing a loan: FHA, VA, USDA or conventional</h2>
<p>The assistance programs sit on top of a regular mortgage, so you still need to pick a loan type. Here is how the main options compare for first-time buyers.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Loan</th><th>Minimum down</th><th>Best for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>FHA</td><td>3.5% with a 580+ score</td><td>Lower credit scores, higher debt ratios</td><td>Upfront and monthly mortgage insurance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conventional</td><td>As low as 3% for first-time buyers</td><td>Good credit; mortgage insurance can be removed later</td><td>Pricing is more sensitive to credit score</td></tr>
<tr><td>VA</td><td>0%</td><td>Eligible veterans, service members and some surviving spouses</td><td>One-time funding fee unless exempt</td></tr>
<tr><td>USDA</td><td>0%</td><td>Eligible rural and some suburban areas, moderate incomes</td><td>Location and household income limits</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>USDA eligibility is set by address, so check the specific property on the USDA eligibility map before you count on it. For a quick read on what payment fits your budget, run your numbers through our <a href="/tools/home-affordability-calculator/">home affordability calculator</a> before you talk to lenders.</p>

<h2>The steps to buying your first home in Texas</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Check your credit and savings.</strong> Pull your credit reports, pay down card balances and set aside cash for earnest money, the option fee, inspections and closing costs.</li>
<li><strong>Get pre-approved.</strong> If you want state assistance, choose a lender that participates in TSAHC or TDHCA programs. Start the homebuyer education course early.</li>
<li><strong>Hire a buyer's agent.</strong> Since January 1, 2026, Texas law requires a written agreement with an agent before they show you homes. It spells out the agent's duties and how they are paid, so read it before you tour.</li>
<li><strong>Shop and make an offer.</strong> Texas agents use contracts promulgated by the Texas Real Estate Commission (TREC). Your offer sets the price, earnest money, option fee, option period length, closing date and who pays for the title policy and survey.</li>
<li><strong>Deliver earnest money and the option fee.</strong> Under the TREC resale contract, both are due to the escrow agent (usually the title company) within 3 days after the effective date.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect during the option period.</strong> Hire a TREC-licensed inspector, plus specialists for the foundation, roof, sewer line or HVAC if the report raises flags.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisal, underwriting and title work.</strong> Your lender orders the appraisal, and the title company searches the property's history and prepares a title commitment for you to review.</li>
<li><strong>Close and file your homestead exemption.</strong> Sign at the title company, get your keys, then apply for the <a href="/blog/texas-homestead-exemption/">Texas homestead exemption</a> to lower your property taxes.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How the Texas option period protects you</h2>
<p>The option period is the most important protection a Texas buyer has, and it surprises people moving from other states. For a negotiated fee paid to the seller, you get the unrestricted right to terminate the contract within a set number of days after the effective date. You do not need a reason.</p>
<p>Key rules from the TREC resale contract:</p>
<ul>
<li>The option fee and number of days are negotiable. In a competitive market, a larger fee or shorter period can make your offer stronger.</li>
<li>If you terminate on time, you lose the option fee but get your earnest money back.</li>
<li>If you close, the option fee is credited to the sales price.</li>
<li>Your termination notice must be delivered by 5:00 p.m. local time on the last day. Time is of the essence, so a late notice does not count.</li>
<li>If you fail to deliver the option fee within 3 days, you lose the unrestricted right to terminate.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use these days for inspections, repair estimates, insurance quotes and a flood risk check. In the Houston area, a flood history can change both your insurance cost and resale value, which our guide to <a href="/blog/buying-a-house-in-a-flood-zone-houston/">buying a house in a flood zone in Houston</a> covers in detail.</p>
[[product:outlet-tester]]

<h2>What it really costs to buy a home in Texas</h2>
<p>Your down payment is only part of the cash you need. Plan for these as well:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Earnest money</strong>, which is applied to your down payment or closing costs at closing.</li>
<li><strong>Option fee</strong>, credited to the price if you close.</li>
<li><strong>Inspections</strong>, paid upfront during the option period.</li>
<li><strong>Appraisal</strong>, often paid upfront to the lender.</li>
<li><strong>Closing costs</strong>, including lender fees, the lender's title policy, escrow fees, recording fees and survey costs.</li>
<li><strong>Prepaids</strong>, including your first year of homeowners insurance and an initial deposit into your escrow account for property taxes and insurance.</li>
</ul>
<p>Texas has no state transfer tax, and title insurance rates are set by the state, which helps keep some costs predictable. Property taxes, on the other hand, are higher than in many states, so build the full escrow amount into your monthly budget. We break down every line item in our guide to <a href="/blog/closing-costs-in-texas/">closing costs in Texas</a>.</p>
[[tool:closing-cost-calculator]]

<h2>Mistakes Texas first-time buyers make</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Skipping the program check.</strong> Many buyers never ask whether they qualify for TSAHC or TDHCA help. Ask every lender you interview.</li>
<li><strong>Waiving the option period to win a bidding war.</strong> It saves a little money and gives up your best exit.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring insurance until the last week.</strong> Homeowners and flood quotes in coastal Texas can swing a budget. Get quotes during the option period.</li>
<li><strong>Opening new credit before closing.</strong> A new car loan or store card can change your debt-to-income ratio and derail underwriting.</li>
<li><strong>Underestimating property taxes.</strong> The seller's exemptions and homestead cap do not transfer to you, so your tax bill and escrow payment can rise after the first year.</li>
</ul>
<p>This guide is general information, not legal or financial advice, so confirm program terms and loan details with a licensed lender or real estate attorney.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>As a first time home buyer in Texas, start by asking a participating lender whether you qualify for TSAHC or TDHCA assistance, then choose the loan that fits your credit and savings. Negotiate an option period you can actually use, budget for closing costs and property taxes, and file your homestead exemption once you move in. Done in that order, buying your first Texas home is very manageable.</p>
